(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_oscillations=[{id:"neet-2026r-q004",pn:4,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Oscillations",st:"SHM velocity-displacement graph",q:"Consider a spring-mass simple harmonic oscillator in one dimension. The mass of the particle is m kg and the spring constant is k Nm\u207B\xB9. At a given instant, the extension of the spring is x-meter and the speed of the particle is v ms\u207B\xB9. On the x - v plane, if the graph of v as a function of x is a circle, then the correct option is:",o:["k = m","k = m\xB2","k = \u221Am","k = 1/m"],c:0,s:`Velocity in SHM: v = \u03C9\u221A(A\xB2 \u2013 x\xB2).
v\xB2 = \u03C9\xB2(A\xB2 \u2013 x\xB2).
v\xB2/\u03C9\xB2 + x\xB2 = A\xB2.
This is the equation of a circle, so \u03C9\xB2 = 1.
k/m = 1.
So k = m.`},{id:"neet-2026-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Energy in SHM",q:"For a simple pendulum, having time period T, the variation of kinetic energy (K.E.) with time (t) is represented by:",o:["K.E. oscillating as a sine-like wave about a dashed mean level above the axis, with marks at T/2 and T","K.E. as a sine wave starting at 0 going positive then negative below the axis, marks at T/2 and T","K.E. as repeated positive humps (always \u2265 0), each hump per half period, with marks at T/2 and T","K.E. a constant horizontal line, mark at T"],c:2,s:`Kinetic energy K = (1/2)mv\xB2.
For SHM, v = A\u03C9 cos(\u03C9t + \u03C6), so K = (1/2)mA\xB2\u03C9\xB2cos\xB2(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
Therefore K \u221D cos\xB2(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
This is never negative and repeats every T/2, so it appears as a series of positive humps (option 3).`,fig:{w:470,h:300,alt:"Four graphs (1)-(4) of kinetic energy K.E. against time t, with T/2 and T marked",d:[["l",60,125,60,25,"a"],["l",60,125,225,125,"a"],["t",57,43,"K.E.","es"],["t",50,138,"0","es"],["t",230,139,"t","s"],["t",12,80,"(1)","s"],["l",60,88,200,88,"d"],["p","M60 88 C82 50 106 50 128 88 C150 126 174 126 196 88"],["t",132,80,"T/2","s"],["t",196,76,"T","s"],["l",196,83,196,93],["l",290,125,290,25,"a"],["l",290,125,455,125,"a"],["t",287,43,"K.E.","es"],["t",280,138,"0","es"],["t",460,139,"t","s"],["t",242,80,"(2)","s"],["p","M290 125 C312 67 336 67 358 125 C380 157 404 157 426 125"],["l",358,121,358,129],["t",360,115,"T/2","s"],["l",426,121,426,129],["t",422,143,"T","s"],["l",60,275,60,175,"a"],["l",60,275,225,275,"a"],["t",57,193,"K.E.","es"],["t",50,288,"0","es"],["t",230,289,"t","s"],["t",12,230,"(3)","s"],["p","M65 275 Q94 211 123 275"],["p","M123 275 Q152 211 181 275"],["p","M181 275 Q192 239 204 255"],["l",123,271,123,279],["t",115,291,"T/2","s"],["l",192,271,192,279],["t",188,291,"T","s"],["l",290,275,290,175,"a"],["l",290,275,455,275,"a"],["t",287,193,"K.E.","es"],["t",280,288,"0","es"],["t",460,289,"t","s"],["t",242,230,"(4)","s"],["l",290,230,425,230],["l",422,271,422,279],["t",418,291,"T","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q034",pn:34,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Floating cylinder oscillation period",q:"A cylindrical cork of uniform density floats in a liquid of density \u03C1\u2081. If the cork is depressed slightly and released, it oscillates harmonically with time period T. If the same cork floats in another liquid of density \u03C1\u2082, then the similar oscillation has time period 2T. The value of \u03C1\u2082 / \u03C1\u2081 is:",o:["2","1/2","1/4","4"],c:2,s:`Time period of a cylinder oscillating in a liquid: T = 2\u03C0\u221A(\u03C1\u209Bl/(\u03C1\u2097g)).
The density \u03C1\u209B of the cork is constant.
So T\u2081/T\u2082 = \u221A(\u03C1\u2082/\u03C1\u2081).
T/(2T) = \u221A(\u03C1\u2082/\u03C1\u2081).
\u03C1\u2082/\u03C1\u2081 = 1/4.`},{id:"neet-2026-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Energy in SHM",q:`The sum of kinetic energy and potential energy of a simple pendulum bob is 0.02 joule. The speed of the simple pendulum bob at equilibrium position is approximately:
(Consider mass of the bob = 20 g)`,o:["1.41 m/s","14.1 m/s","0.2 m/s","2.0 m/s"],c:0,s:`At the equilibrium position the potential energy is zero, so total energy = kinetic energy.
(1/2)mv\xB2 = 0.02.
(1/2) \xD7 20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 v\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.
v\xB2 = 2, so v = \u221A2 = 1.41 m/s.`},{id:"neet-2025-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Spring-mass system, variable mass",q:"In an oscillating spring mass system, a spring is connected to a box filled with sand. As the box oscillates, sand leaks slowly out of the box vertically so that the average frequency \u03C9(t) and average amplitude A(t) of the system change with time t. Which one of the following options schematically depicts these changes correctly?",o:["\u03C9(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value; A(t) stays constant (horizontal line)","\u03C9(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value; A(t) falls with time and flattens at a lower value","\u03C9(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value; A(t) rises with time and flattens at a higher value","\u03C9(t) falls with time and flattens at a lower value; A(t) falls with time and flattens at a lower value"],c:1,s:`At any time the time period is T = 2\u03C0\u221A(m/k).
Here m is decreasing, so the time period T decreases.
Since \u03C9 = 2\u03C0/T, as mass leaks, \u03C9 increases.
Now, at any instant mg = kx\u2080.
So the equilibrium length x\u2080 = mg/k, where m is decreasing.
So the equilibrium length decreases.
So the amplitude also goes on decreasing.`,fig:{w:490,h:207,alt:"Four options, each a pair of graphs of omega(t) above and A(t) below against time t",d:[["l",69,48,69,4.8,"a"],["l",31.8,48,174,48,"a"],["t",43.2,28.2,"\u03C9(t)","s"],["t",177.2,51.9,"t","s"],["p","M69 24 C84 10.8 108 7.8 135 7.8"],["l",69,94.8,69,52.2,"a"],["l",31.8,94.8,174,94.8,"a"],["t",43.2,75,"A(t)","s"],["t",177.2,98.7,"t","s"],["l",69,72,133.8,72],["l",369,48,369,4.8,"a"],["l",331.8,48,474,48,"a"],["t",343.2,28.2,"\u03C9(t)","s"],["t",477.2,51.9,"t","s"],["p","M369 22.8 C384 9.6 408 6.6 433.2 6.6"],["l",369,94.8,369,52.2,"a"],["l",331.8,94.8,474,94.8,"a"],["t",343.2,75,"A(t)","s"],["t",477.2,98.7,"t","s"],["p","M376.2 57 C384 76.8 402 88.8 433.2 88.8"],["l",69,151.2,69,108,"a"],["l",31.8,151.2,174,151.2,"a"],["t",43.2,131.4,"\u03C9(t)","s"],["t",177.2,155.1,"t","s"],["p","M69 127.2 C84 114 108 111 135 111"],["l",69,198,69,155.4,"a"],["l",31.8,198,174,198,"a"],["t",43.2,178.2,"A(t)","s"],["t",177.2,201.9,"t","s"],["p","M69 177 C84 163.2 108 160.2 135 160.2"],["l",369,151.2,369,108,"a"],["l",331.8,151.2,474,151.2,"a"],["t",343.2,131.4,"\u03C9(t)","s"],["t",477.2,155.1,"t","s"],["p","M376.2 111 C384 132 402 145.8 433.2 145.8"],["l",369,198,369,155.4,"a"],["l",331.8,198,474,198,"a"],["t",343.2,178.2,"A(t)","s"],["t",477.2,201.9,"t","s"],["p","M376.2 160.8 C384 180 402 192 433.2 192"],["t",4.2,61.2,"(1)"],["t",304.2,61.2,"(2)"],["t",4.2,165,"(3)"],["t",304.2,165,"(4)"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Spring-mass SHM amplitude",q:"Two identical point masses P and Q, suspended from two separate massless springs of spring constants k\u2081 and k\u2082, respectively, oscillate vertically. If their maximum speeds are the same, the ratio (A_Q/A_P) of the amplitude A_Q of mass Q to the amplitude A_P of mass P is",o:["k\u2082/k\u2081","k\u2081/k\u2082","\u221A(k\u2082/k\u2081)","\u221A(k\u2081/k\u2082)"],c:3,s:`Maximum velocity V = A\u03C9.
v_P = v_Q, so A_P\u03C9_P = A_Q\u03C9_Q.
A_Q/A_P = \u03C9_P/\u03C9_Q, with \u03C9 = \u221A(k/m).
A_Q/A_P = \u221A(k_P m_Q/(m_P k_Q)).
The masses are identical, so A_Q/A_P = \u221A(k\u2081/k\u2082).`},{id:"neet-2024-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"SHM equation",q:"If 5sin(\u03C0t + \u03C0/3) m represents the motion of a particle executing simple harmonic motion, the amplitude and time period of motion, respectively, are;",o:["5 cm, 1 s","5 m, 1 s","5 cm, 2 s","5 m, 2 s"],c:3,s:`x = 5 sin(\u03C0t + \u03C0/3) m.
Amplitude = 5 m.
\u03C9 = \u03C0 = 2\u03C0/T.
T = 2\u03C0/\u03C0 = 2 s.`},{id:"neet-2024-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Simple pendulum time period",q:"If the mass of the bob in a simple pendulum is increased to thrice its original mass and its length is made half its original length, then the new time period of oscillation is x/2 times its original time period. Then the value of x is:",o:["2\u221A3","4","\u221A3","\u221A2"],c:3,s:`Time period T = 2\u03C0\u221A(\u2113/g), which does not depend on the mass.
New length \u2113\u2032 = \u2113/2, so T\u2032 = 2\u03C0\u221A(\u2113/2g).
Given T\u2032 = (x/2) T, so 2\u03C0\u221A(\u2113/2g) = (x/2) \xD7 2\u03C0\u221A(\u2113/g).
This gives 1/\u221A2 = x/2.
So x = \u221A2.`},{id:"neet-2023-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Oscillations",st:"SHM from x-t graph",q:"The x-t graph of a particle performing simple harmonic motion is shown in the figure. The acceleration of the particle at t = 2 s is :",o:["\u2013(\u03C0\xB2/16) ms\u207B\xB2","(\u03C0\xB2/8) ms\u207B\xB2","\u2013(\u03C0\xB2/8) ms\u207B\xB2","(\u03C0\xB2/16) ms\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`From the graph, amplitude A = 1 m and the period is 8 s, so \u03C9 = 2\u03C0/8 = \u03C0/4.
x = sin(\u03C0t/4).
v = (\u03C0/4) cos(\u03C0t/4).
a = \u2212(\u03C0\xB2/16) sin(\u03C0t/4).
At t = 2 s, sin(\u03C0/2) = 1, so a = \u2212\u03C0\xB2/16 m/s\xB2.`,fig:{w:310,h:150,alt:"x\u2013t graph of SHM: from the origin up to x = 1 at t = 2 s, zero at 4 s, minimum x = \u22121 at 6 s, zero at 8 s.",d:[["l",50,140,50,6,"a"],["l",20,75,300,75,"a"],["p","M50 75C64.7 51.4 78.2 30 94 30C109.8 30 123.3 51.4 138 75C152.7 98.6 166.2 120 182 120C197.8 120 211.3 98.6 226 75","B"],["l",50,30,94,30,"d"],["l",94,30,94,75,"d"],["l",50,120,182,120,"d"],["l",182,120,182,75,"d"],["t",44,34,"1","e"],["t",44,124,"\u20131","e"],["t",44,89,"0","e"],["t",94,89,"2","m"],["t",146,70,"4","m"],["t",182,67,"6","m"],["t",230,89,"8","m"],["t",10,63,"x (m)"],["t",296,67,"t (s)","e"]]},sfig:{w:250,h:125,alt:"Sketch of x = sin(\u03C9t): amplitude 1, zero crossings at t = 4 and t = 8.",d:[["l",40,115,40,8,"a"],["l",40,65,230,65,"a"],["p","M40 65C53.3 46.7 65.6 30 80 30C94.4 30 106.7 46.7 120 65C133.3 83.3 145.6 100 160 100C174.4 100 186.7 83.3 200 65","B"],["l",40,30,80,30,"d"],["t",35,34,"1","e"],["t",126,59,"4","m"],["t",202,57,"8","m"],["t",48,12,"x"],["t",226,81,"t","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q006",pn:6,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Damped and simple harmonic motion",q:`Match List-I with List-II:
List-I (x-y graphs): (a) graph a, (b) graph b, (c) graph c, (d) graph d (see figure)
List-II (Situations): (i) Total mechanical energy is conserved; (ii) Bob of a pendulum is oscillating under negligible air friction; (iii) Restoring force of a spring; (iv) bob of a pendulum is oscillating along with air friction
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],c:2,s:`Graph (a) shows oscillations with shrinking amplitude, so it is damped motion: (a) matches (iv).
Graph (b) is a straight line with negative slope through the origin, F = \u2212kx, the spring force: (b) matches (iii).
Graph (c) shows oscillations of constant amplitude, undamped motion: (c) matches (ii).
Graph (d) shows a quarter-circle type curve, the energy-conservation graph: (d) matches (i).`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Four x-y graphs: (a) damped sine wave, (b) straight line of negative slope through the origin, (c) constant-amplitude sine wave, (d) two quarter-circle arcs in a rectangle corner.",d:[["t",4,24,"(a)"],["t",176,24,"(b)"],["t",4,134,"(c)"],["t",176,134,"(d)"],["l",40,90,40,12,"a"],["l",40,90,160,90,"a"],["t",40,8,"y","m"],["t",164,94,"x"],["p","M40 90C43.6 55.4 46.4 55.4 50 90C53.6 117.9 56.4 117.9 60 90C63.6 68.1 66.4 68.1 70 90C73.6 107.3 76.4 107.3 80 90C83.6 76.7 86.4 76.7 90 90C93.6 100.6 96.4 100.6 100 90C103.6 82 106.4 82 110 90"],["l",200,70,324,70,"a"],["l",262,125,262,8,"a"],["t",262,4,"y","m"],["t",328,74,"x"],["l",224,36,300,104],["l",40,192,40,122,"a"],["l",40,192,160,192,"a"],["t",40,118,"y","m"],["t",164,196,"x"],["p","M40 192C44.5 160.1 48 160.1 52.5 192C57 223.9 60.5 223.9 65 192C69.5 160.1 73 160.1 77.5 192C82 223.9 85.5 223.9 90 192C94.5 160.1 98 160.1 102.5 192"],["l",210,205,302,205,"a"],["l",210,205,210,127,"a"],["t",210,123,"y","m"],["t",306,209,"x"],["p","M210 165H254V205"],["p","M210 165A40 40 0 0 1 250 205"],["p","M210 205A40 40 0 0 0 250 165"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q011",pn:11,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Spring restoring force",q:"The restoring force of a spring with a block attached to the free end of the spring is represented by",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:0,s:`The restoring force is F = \u2212kx, a straight line through the origin with negative slope.
Only graph (1) shows this.`,fig:{w:300,h:260,alt:"Four Fx versus x graphs: (1) falling line through origin, (2) rising line through origin, (3) upward parabola with vertex at origin, (4) arch above the x-axis.",d:[["t",2,64,"(1)"],["l",27,60,123,60,"b"],["l",75,112,75,8,"b"],["t",79,10,"F\u2093","s"],["t",127,64,"x"],["l",45,30,105,90],["t",152,64,"(2)"],["l",177,60,273,60,"b"],["l",225,112,225,8,"b"],["t",229,10,"F\u2093","s"],["t",277,64,"x"],["l",195,90,255,30],["t",2,204,"(3)"],["l",27,200,123,200,"b"],["l",75,252,75,148,"b"],["t",79,150,"F\u2093","s"],["t",127,204,"x"],["p","M53 166Q53 200 75 200Q97 200 97 166"],["t",152,204,"(4)"],["l",177,200,273,200,"b"],["l",225,252,225,148,"b"],["t",229,150,"F\u2093","s"],["t",277,204,"x"],["p","M199 200Q199 154 225 154Q251 154 251 200"]]},sfig:{w:150,h:120,alt:"Fx versus x axes with a straight line of negative slope through the origin.",d:[["l",22,60,122,60,"b"],["l",70,112,70,8,"b"],["t",74,10,"F\u2093","s"],["t",126,64,"x"],["l",40,30,100,90]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q020",pn:20,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Periodic functions",q:"Identify the function which represents a non periodic motion.",o:["sin(\u03C9t + \u03C0/4)","e\u207B\u03C9t","sin\u03C9t","sin\u03C9t + cos\u03C9t"],c:1,s:`Sine and cosine functions (and their sums) are harmonic and so periodic.
e\u207B\u03C9t just decays and never repeats, so it is non-periodic.`},{id:"neet-2022-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Time period of a simple pendulum",q:"Two pendulums of length 121 cm and 100 cm start vibrating in phase. At some instant, the two are at their mean position in the same phase. The minimum number of vibrations of the shorter pendulum after which the two are again in phase at the mean position is:",o:["9","10","8","11"],c:3,s:`Time period T = 2\u03C0\u221A(L/g).
They are again in phase when n\u2081T\u2081 = n\u2082T\u2082 with n\u2081, n\u2082 whole numbers.
2\u03C0n\u2081\u221A(1.21/g) = 2\u03C0n\u2082\u221A(1.00/g), so n\u2082/n\u2081 = 11/10.
The shorter pendulum has the smaller period, so it makes more vibrations: n\u2082 = 11 (the longer makes 10).
So the shorter pendulum completes 11 vibrations.`}]})();
